# Source archives — we keep the extracted GEDCOMs in git, not the zips
# No, actually, the zips being all like this. The zips should be ignored individually because it makes it so that, as a human, I'm able to see when the zips are there. 

# out/ IS TRACKED. Emma, 2026-08-15: "Oh my god why the fuck is it gitignored?
# Un gitignore". It used to be `out/*` with two exceptions, and the cost was
# concrete: the Wikidata download-state index lives here, so a machine restart
# lost it and the downloader believed all 514,876 seeds were unfetched while
# 1.4M items sat on disk. Re-running it in that state would have re-requested
# every one of them.
#
# ONLY the two files GitHub physically refuses are ignored, one explicit line
# each — the same convention as the zips, so what is missing stays visible.
# GitHub's hard limit is 100 MB per file and both of these are over it. Both
# are REBUILDABLE from tracked inputs, so this costs a rebuild, never data:
#
#   out/merged.ged                     534 MB  <- `genimerge merge` over exports/
#   out/wikidata/download-state.sqlite3 126 MB  <- `wikidata-download --rebuild-index`
#
# out/wikidata/store-index.sqlite3 is 99.9 MB and tracked. It is close enough to
# the limit that the next growth spurt will cross it; when a push is rejected for
# that file, add a line here and say so rather than widening this to a pattern.
out/merged.ged
out/wikidata/download-state.sqlite3

